  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure to our northeast remains the big player in our\nweather for the next several days. Dry conditions with a gradual\nwarming trend are the only main points for the forecast. Areas\nof river valley fog will occur each morning, but widespread\nformation looks low confidence. \n\nPleasant and warm temperatures across the forecast area can be\nexpected each afternoon through the week. The lowlands will see\nhighs in the upper 60s to mid 70s, with the mountains remaining\nin the 50s to upper 60s. Overnight lows will be chilly due to a\nvery dry airmass overhead with 30s and 40s being the norm\nuntil Tuesday night. Areas of patchy frost will be likely across\nthe mountains as a result of these temperatures each morning \nthrough Tuesday.\n\nMinimum RH values in the 30s and 40s will be common for many\nlocations each afternoon due to very dry dew points and warm\nafternoon temperatures. Fire weather is low risk though due to\ncalm to light winds with the stagnant air mass. \n\nNext chances of rain are not expected until late next Saturday \nwith a possible cold front moving through the area. Even then \nmodels do not have a decent handle on the timing quite yet. Dry \nand pleasant weather will remain in place until then.",
